                "TOCHeading": "Environmental Fate/Exposure Summary",
                "Description": "This section summarizes the environmental fate/exposure of this chemical, including the natural and artificial sources of the chemical as a pollutant, its environmental fate, biodegradation, abiotic degradation, bioconcentration, etc.",
                "Information": [
                  {
                    "ReferenceNumber": 53,
                    "Description": "PEER REVIEWED",
                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Palmitic acid's production and use in the manufacture of soaps, lubricating oils, and waterproofing materials and in the synthesis of metal palmitates and food-grade additives will result in its release to the environment through various waste streams. Palmitic acid is a common saturated fatty acid occurring in natural fats and in oils. Fatty acids are an important part of the daily diet of mammals, birds and invertebrates. Palmitic acid is a constituent of various plant parts, tissues, and essential oils of a variety of plant species; it has been identified as a volatile constituent of Korean chamchwi plant and nectarine. If released to air, a vapor pressure of 3.8X10-7 mm Hg at 25 °C indicates palmitic acid will exist in both the vapor and particulate phases in the atmosphere. Vapor-phase palmitic acid will be degraded in the atmosphere by reaction with photochemically-produced hydroxyl radicals; the half-life for this reaction in air is estimated to be 20 hours. Particulate-phase palmitic acid will be removed from the atmosphere by wet or dry deposition. Palmitic acid does not contain chromophores that absorb at wavelengths >290 nm and, therefore, is not expected to be susceptible to direct photolysis by sunlight. If released to soil, neutral palmitic acid is expected to have no mobility based upon an estimated Koc of 1.3X10+4. The estimated pKa of palmitic acid is 4.95, indicating that this compound will exist almost entirely in anion form in the environment and anions generally do not adsorb more strongly to soils containing organic carbon and clay than their neutral counterparts. Volatilization from moist soil is not expected because the compound exists as an anion and anions do not volatilize. Palmitic acid is not expected to volatilize from dry soil surfaces based upon its vapor pressure. Utilizing a modified OECD screening test accounting for poor water solubility, palmitic acid reached 84% of its theoretical BOD in 28 days, suggesting that biodegradation is expected to be an important environmental fate process under certain environmental conditions in soil. If released into water, neutral palmitic acid is expected to adsorb to suspended solids and sediment based upon the estimated Koc. The concentration of palmitic acid decreased from 536 to 3.7 ug/L (half-life approximately 0.50 days) during a 29-day incubation period of freshwater, suggesting that biodegradation may be an important environmental fate process in water under certain conditions. An estimeted pKa of 4.95 indicates palmitic acid will exist almost entirely in the anion form at pH values of 5 to 9 and, therefore, volatilization from water surfaces is not expected to be an important fate process. An experiemntal BCF of 60 suggests bioconcentration in aquatic organisms is moderate. Hydrolysis is not expected to be an important environmental fate process since this compound lacks functional groups that hydrolyze under environmental conditions (pH 5 to 9). Occupational exposure to palmitic acid may occur through inhalation of powder and dermal contact with this compound at workplaces where palmitic acid is produced or used. Monitoring data indicate that the general population may be exposed to palmitic acid via inhalation of ambient air, ingestion of food and drinking water, and dermal contact with consumer products containing palmitic acid. (SRC)",
