                    "Value": {
                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Palmitic acid is a saturated fatty acid which occurs in natural fats, oils, and tall oil(1). Palmitic acid occurs as the glyceryl ester in fats and oils(2). Palmitic acid can be obtained from Japan wax or Chinese vegetable tallow(2). Palmitic acid was determined to be a volatile constituent from the Korean chamchwi plant(Aster scaber Thunb)(3). Palmitic acid has been identified as a volatile constituent of nectarine(4). Mature grains of brown rice were found to contain palmitic acid(5). Palmitic acid was found in paprika oleoresin at <0.1 mg/kg(6). This compound is a carboxylic acid that is also known as a fully saturated long chain fatty acid; fatty acids were first isolated by the hydrolysis of naturally occurring fats(7). Fatty acids are widely distributed in nature as components of animal and vegetable fats(8) including lipids such as oils and fats, waxes, sterol esters and other minor compounds(7,8). Palmitic acid is reported found in apple, celery, in the essential oil of lemon and sweet orange, tomato, banana, cranberry, guava, grapes, melon, papaya, pear, raspberry, strawberry, cinnamon, ginger, saffron, hop oil, soybean, avocado, plums, beans, mushroom, starfruit, marjoram, fenugreek, mango, tamarind, fig, kelp, cardamom, rice, prickly pear, dill, licorice, and sweet grass oil(9). Palmitic acid is a constituent of various plant parts, tissues, and essential oils of a variety of plant species(10).",
