                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "An average palmitic acid concentration of 6,500 ng/ul was identified in an industrial wastewater survey in which samples collected between Nov 1, 1979 to Nov 1, 1981 were analyzed(1). Water samples from three secondary treatment publicly owned treatment works in NJ were found to contain average palmitic acid concentrations of 14, 8, and 25 ppb(2). Secondary effluents from ten municipal and industrial wastewater treatment plants discharging into Illinois rivers were sampled; palmitic acid was identified in effluents from St. Charles Public Owned Treatment Work (POTW), Addison POTW, and Bensenville POTW(3). Palmitic acid was found in straw pulp bleaching and combined mill effluents taken during a production week starting on May 20, 1983 in Denmark(4). The average concentration of palmitic acid identified in Los Angeles County effluent collected quarterly from Nov 1980 to Aug 1981 was determined to be 2,500 ug/L(5). Wastewater from two kraft paper mills in Georgia were sampled in March 1972 and found to contain approximately 0.218 and 0.088 mg/L palmitic acid (as the methyl derivative)(6). Palmitic acid has been identified in unknown concentrations of leachate from a sanitary landfill in Barcelona, Spain(7). Palmitic acid has been identified in leachate samples collected from the interior of a municipal landfill situated in Gryta, Vasteras, Sweden in May 1990, concentration unknown(8). Palmitic acid was found in sludges from a Finnish pulp and paper mill(9). Palmitic acid was found in landfill leachate from a municipal solid waste facility in Porto Alegre City, Brazil(10). Palmitic acid was identified in Iona Island Sewage Treatment Plant (British Columbia, Canada) sewage and sludge effluent, concentrations ranging from 229 to 1,345 ug/L(11). Palmitic acid has been identified in sewage treatment effluent water samples from the lower River Lee, North of London, England in large quantities (> 0.1 ug/L)(12). Effluents from two pulp mills located downstream of the Township of Kawerau, in the Eastern Bay of Plenty region, New Zealand were found to contain an average of 311 and 20.5 ppb palmitic acid(13). Palmitic acid was identified in  the emissions of a municipal waste incineration plant in Germany as having a concentration of 36.7 ug/cu m(14). Groundwater and soil from 91 waste sites at 18 Department of Energy (DOE) facilities were analyzed; palmitic acid was found at one of the facilities in groundwater, concentration not quantified(15).",
