                      "StringWithMarkup": [
                        {
                          "String": "Fine particle aerosol emission rates for palmitic acid from noncatalyst automobiles, catalyst automobiles, and heavy-duty diesel trucks were determined to be 6.0, 63.9, and 153.1 ug/km, respectively(1). Palmitic acid was identified in tire wear particles, brake lining particles, and road dust particles at concentrations of 4,818.4, 83.3, and 1,216.7 ug/g of particle sample, respectively(2). Palmitic acid was found in fine particulate abrasion products from green leaves at a concentration of 835.4 ug/g and from dead leaves at a concentration of 589.9 ug/g; samples collected were from trees characteristic of the Los Angeles, CA area(3). Fine particle emission rates for palmitic acid from a natural gas-fired water heater and a natural gas-fired space heater were determined(4). A HEPA-filtered dilution air sample emission rate for palmitic acid was determined to be 9.7 pg/kJ; the emission rate for palmitic acid through the first filter was determined to be 310.4 pg/kJ; the emission rate for palmitic acid through the backup filter was 56.8 pg/kJ(4). The emission rate for palmitic acid from motor vehicles in the Van Nuys tunnel in Los Angeles at 493.4 mg/L of gasoline-equivalent fuel burned, sampled on Sept 21, 1993(4). Palmitic acid has been identified in the influent (35.91 ug/L) and effluent (0.71 ug/L) from the Kallby STP located by the Hoje River, south of Lund, Sweden(5).",
