                          "String": "Palmitic acid was identified in vapor with a concn range of 40 to 300 ng/cu m and on particles with a concn of 40 to 270 ng/g emitted during combustion of coal at Ames power plant in Iowa(1). Palmitic acid was found in candle smoke from paraffin and beeswax at 18.09 and 8.29 mg/g of organic compounds(2). Palmitic acid was found in wood smoke from red maple(3), red oak(3), white birch(3), white pine(3), hemlock(3), balsam fir(3), yellow poplar(4), white ash(4), sweet gum(4), mockernut hickory(4), loblolly pine(4), and slash pine(4). Palmitic acid was detected in wood smoke from pine, oak and synthetic logs at 13.91, 21.46 and 10.11 mg/kg of wood burnt(5). Palmitic acid was found at 19.9, 16.9 and 15.4 mg/kg of burnt pine, oak and eucalyptus wood in the particulate phase, respectively, and at 2.00 mg/kg of burnt pine wood in the gas phase(6). Palmitic acid was found in extract from a pine wood burning campfire at 460 ng/g(7). Palmitic acid was found in the fine aerosols from boilers burning number 2 distillate fuel oil(8). Palmitic acid was found at 2492.0 ug/g from heated roofing tar pot fumes(9). Palmitic acid was found in gas and particulate matter effluents from commercial-scale meat charbroiling operations at 11,400 and 163,000 ug/kg meat cooked, respectively(10). Average concentrations of palmitic acid emitted during Chinese cooking practices were reported as 10,608, 30,121, 23,344, and 14,757 ng/mg, for Cantonese style, Sichuan style, Dongbei style, and Hunan style cooking, respectively(11).",
